# gitleaks finding fingerprints suppressed as verified false positives.
#
# Format: <commit>:<path>:<rule-id>:<line>
#
# This file suppresses ONE finding each, pinned to the exact commit, file, rule
# and line. It is deliberately not a path or rule allowlist: every rule still
# runs everywhere, and a real secret on an adjacent line is still caught.
#
# Prefer an inline `gitleaks:allow` comment for new code. A fingerprint is only
# needed when the finding lives in a commit diff that already exists in history,
# because a comment added later cannot change what an old commit's diff says.

# pg_dump's `--restrict-key` flag, given a fixed 32-character hex dummy, in the
# schema-dump test helper. It is a pg_dump 18+ option, not a credential, and the
# value is fixed on purpose: without it pg_dump emits a RANDOM \restrict key per
# run, so two dumps of identical schemas would differ and the convergence
# assertion (`assert_eq!(upgrade_dump, fresh_dump)`) could never hold. Matched by
# the low-confidence `generic-api-key` rule on the `key=<hex>` shape alone.
#
# Do not quote the literal value in this file. Doing so once put the same string
# on an added line of a NEW commit, which the scanner then flagged in turn --
# this file is scanned like any other.
